          "type": "turn",
          "question": "At what blood pressure reading is hypertension diagnosed, and do the major bodies agree?",
          "answer": "**Short answer: the major guidelines do not fully agree.** The threshold depends on which guidance you follow.\n\nAccording to the hypertension diagnosis summary (guideline-hypertension-diagnosis-and-bp-targets), the 130/80 mm Hg threshold is the United States position. European guidance says high blood pressure in the office starts at 140/90 mm Hg. It treats readings of 130 to 139 over 80 to 89 as \"high-normal\" rather than stage 1 disease. The same reading can therefore be labeled differently, and the number of people counted as hypertensive is substantially larger under the US threshold.\n\nThe treatment summary (guideline-hypertension-first-line-treatment) adds that the two bodies also differ on how aggressively to treat low-risk stage 1 hypertension. Under European guidance, many people whom US guidance calls stage 1 are advised on lifestyle changes alone.\n\nThe retrieved passages do not give the full diagnostic criteria for either body, such as how many readings are needed or how home readings are counted. They also do not name the specific organizations. For a personal reading, a clinician can say which guidance applies and what it means for you.\n\nNot medical advice. This is an educational software project. It does not diagnose or treat, and it must not be used for real medical decisions.",
